    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d655">

                <pb facs="1058" n="1040" sameAs="frus:frus1948v03/pg1040"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">852.00/6–2948</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum of Conversation, by Mr. William B. Dunham of
                        the Division of Western European Affairs</hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">confidential</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1948-06-29">June 29, 1948</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <table>
                    <row role="label">
                        <cell>Participants:</cell>
                        <cell>Mr. Baraibar, Chargé d’Affaires of the Spanish Embassy</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell/>
                        <cell>Mr. Achilles, Chief, WE </cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell/>
                        <cell>Mr. Dunham, WE </cell>
                    </row>
                </table>
                <p>During his call this morning, Mr. Baraibar was shown a copy of a telegram<anchor
                        n="1" xml:id="d655-f1"/> from Mr. Culbertson in which he reported that he
                    had been informed by the Foreign Minister that the Spanish Government expects to
                    announce within the next few weeks the holding of municipal elections in Spain
                    sometime during October or November.</p>
                <p>Mr. Achilles said this was a very hopeful sign and he hoped the Spanish
                    Government would go through with this action. He felt that any measures taken by
                    the Spanish Government to dramatize the elections would be helpful in indicating
                    to the world that Spain, in its own way and its own time, was moving in the
                    direction of more liberal domestic policies. He mentioned to Mr. Baraibar that
                    any of these actions taken before the forthcoming General Assembly in Paris
                    would undoubtedly have a beneficial effect.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
